  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 AM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nBig picture pattern for the first half of the week is a ridge \nof high pressure centered over the southern Rockies which is \nsending monsoon moisture our way. Meanwhile, an upper level \ntrough is pressing into the Pacific Northwest. We're in the \nmiddle between these two features. This keeps a threat for \nafternoon and evening storms going through the first half of the\nweek with the southeast highlands with the better chances for \nisolated to scattered storms. We also remain mild if not hot \nwith temps in the mid 90s and possibly approaching to 100 as the\nNBM has been underperforming on highs the past few days. So the\nheat advisory continues through today at least. Expect breezy \nconditions across the central mountains into the upper Snake \nRiver Plain with the upper trough trying to press into the \nridge. We are watching smoke potential from the Oregon \nwildfires. HRRR smoke model shows some smoke moving back into \nthe Magic Valley this morning and retreating for the afternoon. \nPotentially, a bigger surge of near surface smoke for Monday \nwhich could extend into the Snake Plain. \n\nLooks like the trough passes through Thursday and the monsoonal flow \ndiminishes. We still remain under the influence of the ridge for \nlate week into the weekend. But it looks drier with fewer clouds so \nthat may allow our temps to rise back towards 100, assuming they \nback off some early to mid week.",
